ncome": "S$10m", "Date of birth / age": "45 now", "Employment / income source": "Salary"}}, "modelMessage": "", "rmSignedDate": "2026-10-26", "threeLStatus": "ok", "threeLMessage": "Drafted the 3L allocation from the meeting notes.", "rmSignatureImage": "/signature-isabel.png", "threeLProportion": null, "threeLProportionMessage": ""}, "parts": [{"body": "Return Requirements: The client wants her assets to grow steadily towards the 700M target within 8 years\\nReturn Requirements: They want the portfolio to reach 800M within 5 years\\nInvestment Time Horizon: The client expects to stay invested for at least ten years\\nInvestment Time Horizon: They would not need to touch most of the capital for at least ten years, so she is a long-term investor\\nPrimary Goals: Keeping her capital safe comes first and steady income is second\\nPrimary Goals: They want the portfolio to reach 700M within 8 years\\nPrimary Goals: They want the portfolio to reach 800M within 5 years\\nSecondary Goals: The client wants to leave something for her family and perhaps support education charities later\\nSecondary Goals: After that, she would like her assets to grow, but only modestly and without an aggressive approach\\nLiquidity Needs: The client spends about S$150,000 a month on living costs and wishes to keep a cash reserve of S$30m\\nTax Considerations: The client is a Singapore tax resident in the top income tax bracket of 24% and prefers low turnover\\nUnique Circumstances and Preferences: She wants low risk, but steady growth", "part": 1, "owner": "rm", "title": "Client goals and objectives", "status": "filled"}, {"body": "Liquidity: Strategy A (Fixed Income). The client requires a S$30m cash reserve for immediate needs, with no large purchases planned in the next few years. Given the low risk tolerance and short-term focus, Strategy A (Fixed Income) is appropriate as its maximum drawdown of 15% and longest drawdown of 4 years align with the need for capital preservation. Longevity: Strategy B (Income). For the longevity bucket, the client's primary focus remains on safety and income, preferring government bonds and high-grade bonds. Strategy B (Income) offers a modest expected return of 3.0% while maintaining low volatility and a maximum drawdown of 15%, which is suitable for supporting lifestyle needs over time. Legacy: Strategy C (Yield). While the client has a long horizon (at least ten years) and wishes to leave wealth for family/charity, safety remains the top priority. Strategy C (Yield) provides slightly more growth potential than B while keeping the maximum drawdown at 25% and the longest drawdown at 4 years, balancing modest returns with acceptable risk.", "part": 2, "owner": "pm", "title": "3L buckets and explanation", "status": "filled"}, {"body": "A fall of more than 10% in a single year would make her very uncomfortable and would likely prompt her to sell", "part": 3, "owner": "rm", "title": "Client risk profile", "status": "filled"}, {"body": "", "part": 4, "owner": "pm", "title": "Strategic Asset Allocation", "status": "empty"}, {"body": "Review frequency: The IPS is reviewed at least annually, and the portfolio is monitored against it on an ongoing basis.\\nReview triggers: An ad-hoc review is held on a material change in the client's circumstances (income, wealth, health, family, liabilities), on a major life event (for example retirement, a property purchase or a child starting university), on a significant market event, when the portfolio deviates from the Strategic Asset Allocation beyond the agreed tolerance bands, or on a regulatory change that affects the mandate.\\nWho reviews: The Relationship Manager leads the review with the client; the Portfolio Manager confirms the allocation and the strategies; compliance sign-off is required for any change to the IPS.\\nDocumentation: Every review and every change is recorded as a new IPS version with its date, author, the reason for the change and the supporting meeting note.\\nCommunication to the client: The client receives the updated IPS after each review, and is told promptly of any breach of the tolerance bands or any change to the strategy; the client confirms it in writing.\\n\\n3L's Strategic Asset Allocations\\nLiquidity portfolio: Strategy A - Fixed Income. The client requires a S$30m cash reserve for immediate needs, with no large purchases planned in the next few years. Given the low risk tolerance and short-term focus, Strategy A (Fixed Income) is appropriate as its maximum drawdown of 15% and longest drawdown of 4 years align with the need for capital preservation.\\nLongevity portfolio: Strategy B - Income. For the longevity bucket, the client's primary focus remains on safety and income, preferring government bonds and high-grade bonds. Strategy B (Income) offers a modest expected return of 3.0% while maintaining low volatility and a maximum drawdown of 15%, which is suitable for supporting lifestyle needs over time.\\nLegacy portfolio: Strategy C - Yield. While the client has a long horizon (at least ten years) and wishes to leave wealth for family/charity, safety remains the top priority. Strategy C (Yield) provides slightly more growth potential than B while keeping the maximum drawdown at 25% and the longest drawdown at 4 years, balancing modest returns with acceptable risk.", "part": 5, "owner": "rm", "title": "Review rules", "status": "filled"}, {"body": "Signed by the RM on 2026-10-26.", "part": 6, "owner": "rm", "title": "Signature line", "status": "filled"}], "author": "Relationship Manager", "sentAt": "2026-10-27T12:35:28+00:00", "status": "Sent to PM", "summary": "AI-generated draft from meeting notes", "version": 1, "clientId": "jft", "issuedOn": "2026-10-26"}
\.


